# Dual remote push (GitHub + GitLab)

After finishing code/content changes in this project (when work is ready to save remotely), **always commit (if needed) and push to both remotes** without waiting for the user to ask again.

## Remotes

| Remote | URL | Branch |
|--------|-----|--------|
| `origin` (GitHub) | `https://github.com/arunsaikumarB/dream-home-builder.git` | `main` (or current tracking branch) |
| `gitlab` | `http://gitlab.lstech-hq.lstechinc.com/websites/dh-builders.git` | `dh-builder-v2` |

## Workflow

1. Commit meaningful changes when the task is complete (or when the user asked to push). Follow the project's commit message style.
2. Push to GitHub: `git push -u origin HEAD` (or the tracked branch, usually `main`).
3. Push the same commit(s) to GitLab branch `dh-builder-v2`:
   - `git push -u gitlab HEAD:dh-builder-v2`
4. If `gitlab` remote is missing, add it first:
   - `git remote add gitlab http://gitlab.lstech-hq.lstechinc.com/websites/dh-builders.git`
5. **Never force-push** (`--force`, `-f`, `--force-with-lease`) to any branch or remote — even if asked. Use normal pushes only.
6. Do not update git config. Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials).

## Notes

- Prefer project `.git` under this workspace — never operate on a parent/home git repo by mistake.
- Skip empty commits when there is nothing to push.
- Report both remote URLs/status briefly after a successful push.
